WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 9.5 · Financial Management
Cash flow, forecasting and runway

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Start-up costs come first and income later: forecast your cash monthly and know your runway.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:27.763
A cash flow forecast shows, month by month, the cash you expect to receive and pay out, and what is left. Runway is how many months you could keep going if no new income arrived.

4
00:00:28.163 --> 00:00:28.763
How the lines connect

5
00:00:28.763 --> 00:00:38.981
Opening balance: Cash in the bank at the start of the month. For example: Our bakery starts January with US$4,000 of the founders’ savings in its account.

6
00:00:38.981 --> 00:00:51.422
Receipts and payments: Money actually arriving and leaving, not money invoiced or promised. For example: In: US$3,200 from stall and café sales, US$500 from the youth fund. Out: US$4,000 for rent, flour and pay.

7
00:00:51.422 --> 00:01:02.381
Closing balance: What is left at the end of the month; it becomes next month’s opening balance. For example: US$4,000 + US$3,700 − US$4,000 leaves US$3,700 to start February.

8
00:01:02.381 --> 00:01:12.689
Runway: Closing balance divided by your average monthly payments. For example: US$3,700 ÷ US$4,000 a month gives just under one month of runway.

9
00:01:13.089 --> 00:01:13.689
Why cash comes first

10
00:01:13.689 --> 00:01:18.993
1. A surplus on paper does not pay bills; cash does.

11
00:01:18.993 --> 00:01:24.667
2. Start-up costs come first and income later, so gaps are likely.

12
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:31.341
3. It shows a shortfall months ahead, while you can still act.

13
00:01:31.741 --> 00:01:32.341
How you will use it

14
00:01:32.341 --> 00:01:37.644
1. Monthly: replace estimates with actual figures and check your runway.

15
00:01:37.644 --> 00:01:43.319
2. Scenarios: test late sales, late payments or a failed funding bid.

16
00:01:43.319 --> 00:01:49.993
3. Triggers: a minimum runway that sets off actions agreed in advance.

17
00:01:50.393 --> 00:01:50.993
What to avoid

18
00:01:50.993 --> 00:01:55.926
Counting invoices as cash before the money actually arrives.

19
00:01:55.926 --> 00:02:00.489
Building the forecast once and never updating it.

20
00:02:00.489 --> 00:02:07.533
Waiting for a crisis to decide what you would delay or cut.

21
00:02:08.133 --> 00:02:09.933
You draft a twelve-month cash forecast and set your runway trigger.

22
00:02:09.933 --> 00:02:13.933
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:15.433 --> 00:02:18.533
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
